  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Life-threatening flash flooding ongoing and\ncontinues to be possible today and tonight with numerous rounds\nof showers and thunderstorms expected. \n\nSignificant flash flooding occurred overnight over portions of \nNW NC and SW VA, including the Mount Airy and Pilot Mountain \nareas. This includes water rescues, cars caught in flood waters,\nand water inundating roads and buildings. Mesoanalysis shows a \nstrong southeasterly LLJ interacting with the higher terrain \nacross the area in the vicinity of a warm front. Very high \nrainfall rates have been indicative of this activity, with \nrainfall totals of 6-10+ inches in some areas.\n\nThe increased lifting for ascent as the trough axis approaches \ntoday and tonight will trigger additional showers and storms. \nThe strong SSE upslope flow will continue to enhance the rain \nefficiency and support cell regeneration, leading to a continued\nhigh chance for flash flooding, especially along higher \nterrain. A few cells this afternoon and evening may also contain\ndamaging winds. \n\nPer coordination with neighboring offices and with guidance from\nWPC, have extended the Flood Watch to all counties in NW NC, SW\nVA, and SE WV in our area along and northwest of a Pilot Mountain,\nNC to Lynchburg, VA line through early Monday morning for \ninstances of flash flooding.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather continues through much of the\nweek.\n \nThe closed low will gradually open up as it moves from near \nIL/IN towards the TN Valley and Mid Atlantic region through \nMonday. Models have the base vorticity maximum cutting off \nover the Deep South by Tuesday morning, remaining in the region \nand continuing to help trigger showers and thunderstorms. At the\nsurface, surface low pressure lifts northeast from IL/IN but \nstrong southerly flow and a NE to SW oriented front stalls in \nthe forecast area, at least for Tuesday into Wednesday. This \nwill continue to enhance rainfall rates and pose mostly a flash \nflood threat, although the occasional strong cell with damaging \nwinds will be possible. After that, we still have daily\nafternoon and evening rain chances, with no real a FROPA \nbringing an end to the influx of moisture.",
